+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md for motivation. Current cart and order population paths resolve product associations during reads, while order lines already represent the values that must remain historical. Deletion must therefore be isolated to active catalog and cart resolution paths.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- Make cart reads and mutations tolerate missing catalog products and persist the cleaned cart.
+- Ensure checkout pricing and line creation consume only live cart items.
+- Make order reads and status updates rely on persisted order-line data rather than reloading deleted products.
+- Preserve normal 404 behavior for deleted product lookup and normal behavior for unrelated products.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- Recovering or soft-deleting catalog products.
+- Changing historical order totals or retroactively repricing orders.
+- Changing deletion behavior for unrelated catalog entities.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Treat cart product absence as removable state.** Filter missing product references at the cart service/facade boundary, delete their cart items, and recalculate from the survivors. This keeps all cart APIs and checkout consistent; changing only one controller would leave alternate mutation paths unsafe.
+- **Use order-line snapshots as the historical source.** Populate order DTOs and status operations from stored order product fields and tolerate a missing catalog association. This is preferable to retaining catalog rows, which would make deletion impossible, and to reconstructing history from the mutable product table.
+- **Keep product lookup catalog-backed.** A missing product remains a 404, while cart/order code handles absence explicitly. This preserves the API contract and avoids exposing deleted products for new purchases.
+- **Add integration coverage at the API boundary.** Exercise deletion followed by cart reads/mutations/checkout, merchant and customer order reads/status, and v2 lookup, including the cart-only-deleted edge case.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Risk] Existing lazy relationships may still throw during serialization → guard product resolution in every relevant populator and use persisted order-line values when the catalog entity is absent.
+- [Risk] Cart cleanup may not be persisted consistently → perform cleanup through the existing cart service transaction before returning or checking out.
+- [Risk] Fixture setup may hide missing-product behavior → explicitly delete the catalog product in integration tests before each read or checkout assertion.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+No database migration is expected if order product snapshot columns are already persisted. Deploy code and run the focused integration suite; rollback is a code-only rollback.
